{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310734,"nonce":"rehSmFnRENuUY+ym2mT4VLv3ng4=","title":"The tiered backend's upgrade_to_llvm prints success while every recorded pointer holds zeroed memory — the tiered benchmark targets never ...","message":"**RESOLVED b41e952a — and the headline claim was WRONG.** Read the correction\nbefore using anything below.\n\nThe all-zero dump is a DUMP-TIMING ARTIFACT, not evidence. The dump runs inside\n`upgrade_to_llvm` before MCJIT finalises; the pointers are real, the\nno-downgrade guard in `install_beadie_pointer` is correct (`current \u003e\ntarget_tier` returns), the LLVM pointer stays installed, and execution really\ndoes run LLVM code — if it did not, calling zeroed memory would crash instead\nof completing. So \"the tiered targets never execute LLVM\" was FALSE.\n\nThe real cause of the 2.6x was AArch64 codegen, fixed in b41e952a: LLVM's\nload/store optimiser forms `ldp` across a field written by the previous\niteration, and store-to-load forwarding cannot serve a paired load that\noverlaps a narrower recent store. `-aarch64-enable-ldst-opt=false` takes nbody\nstandalone LLVM 1581ms -\u003e 609ms, ahead of Cranelift's 635ms. Neutral on\nkernels without the shape. See\n[[perf_llvm_jit_intrinsic_wrappers_and_sqrt_trap]] — same mechanism as the\n`fsqrt` trap, which is why inlining sqrt looked like the culprit there.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY DISCRIMINATED: fibonacci showed LLVM FASTEST (262 vs cranelift\n302), which killed \"constant harness overhead\" and pointed at a kernel-shape\nissue. Reach for a second kernel early.\n\nOriginal (superseded) writeup follows.\n\nOPEN, **macOS/aarch64 ONLY**. Found 2026-08-14 while asking why `rayzor-llvm`\nlooked far slower than `rayzor-tiered` on Mac.\n\nPLATFORM SPLIT — verified by running the same probe on both boxes:\n\n| box | pointers recorded by upgrade_to_llvm |\n|---|---|\n| Mac M1 Pro | `main`/`advance`/`energy`/`new` all **32 KB of ZEROS** |\n| NUC i5-1250P (Linux x86_64) | all four hold **real code**, 46-48 KB nonzero |\n\nSo Linux/CI tiered numbers ARE LLVM numbers and are not affected. Do not\ngeneralise the Mac symptom to CI — it was checked and is not there. Both boxes\ntake the MCJIT path (the AOT-to-dylib route is behind `RAYZOR_LLVM_TIER_AOT`),\nso the suspicion is MCJIT on aarch64 macOS handing back an address whose page\nis not populated — W^X / lazy emission.\n\nUnexplained on Linux and NOT the same bug: tiered 495 ms sits above BOTH\ncranelift 388 and llvm 402, so the tiered harness carries ~90 ms/iteration of\nits own overhead there (`reset_loaded_modules_for_run` + dispatch), which is a\nharness cost, not codegen.\n\n`TieredBackend::upgrade_to_llvm` → `compile_all_with_llvm` returns Ok, prints\n`[LLVM] Upgrade succeeded`, and registers pointers. `RAYZOR_DUMP_MACHINE_CODE`\ndumps the bytes at each recorded pointer. For nbody, **every** NBody function —\n`main`, `advance`, `energy`, `new` — dumps as 32 KB of ZEROS.\n\nCorroborated independently by timing, which is the stronger half of the\nargument (a zero dump alone could be a lazy MCJIT stub not yet emitted at dump\ntime, since the dump runs immediately after compile):\n\n| target | nbody (Mac M1 Pro) | what it really runs |\n|---|---|---|\n| rayzor-cranelift | 629 ms | Cranelift |\n| rayzor-tiered | 596-614 ms | Cranelift — it tracks cranelift, not llvm |\n| rayzor-precompiled-tiered | 601 ms | same |\n| **rayzor-llvm** | **1634 ms** | genuinely LLVM |\n\nIf the tiered targets executed the LLVM code they claim to have installed, they\nwould land near 1634, not near 629.\n\n**So the tiered \"LLVM gain\" is an illusion — those targets never execute LLVM\ncode.** The only target that measures LLVM is the standalone `rayzor-llvm`,\nwhich builds `LLVMJitBackend` directly: no TieredBackend, so no beadie, no\ninterpreter warmup and no Cranelift anywhere in the path.\n\nWhich means the real, uncomfortable reading: **on aarch64 rayzor's LLVM\ncodegen is ~2.6x SLOWER than its Cranelift codegen for nbody.** On x86_64 the\ntwo are at parity (NUC i5-1250P: llvm 402 ms vs cranelift 388 ms), so this is\nan aarch64 problem, not an LLVM-tier problem in general. Suspect the same\nApple-silicon shape as\n[[perf_llvm_jit_intrinsic_wrappers_and_sqrt_trap]]: LLVM pairs loads into\n`ldp` across a field written on the previous iteration and store-to-load\nforwarding fails every iteration.\n\nRULED OUT as the cause of the standalone/tiered gap — do not re-derive:\n- Different LLVM configuration. Both call `LLVMJitBackend::with_symbols`, whose\n  opt level defaults to Aggressive (O3).\n- Different MIR. Both use `CompilationConfig::default()`, `PassManager::\n  for_level(OptimizationLevel::O2)` and `strip_stack_trace_updates`.\n- Per-iteration re-initialisation in `call_main`. MEASURED with\n  `RAYZOR_LLVM_TRACE_STARTUP=1`: 4 init calls per iteration (3\n  `__vtable_init__` + 1 `__init__`), nowhere near a second.\n\nTRAPS hit while chasing this:\n- `RAYZOR_DUMP_MACHINE_CODE=` with an EMPTY value dumps NOTHING (the guard is\n  `if want.is_empty() || !name.contains(want)  { continue }`), which reads as\n  \"the dump never fires\".\n- A run from the wrong cwd fails with \"No such file or directory\" and every\n  grep over its output comes back empty — which reads as non-determinism. Check\n  the binary exists before believing an empty result. Same class as the\n  grep-swallowed-the-error trap in\n  [[bugs_interpreter_ffi_passes_floats_in_int_registers]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_tiered_llvm_upgrade_reports_success_but_pointers_are_empty` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}